 to bottom and it sort  of chops everything  underneath there  it's a bit more aggressive to use but  very effective for these big brumbles  and then you also recently had it play  with this blade  it feels very rough to me it's just a  circular saw blade  um just spinning there in front of you i  guess i'm used to working with table  saws where everything is very fixed and  safe and protection covers and here you  are just swinging with a blade but this  mainly used to chop more smaller trees  and branches in front of you so you can  just sort of have a yeah i guess a saw  in front of you chopping the things  the blade we use mainly is this one  because a bit of an all-rounder you can  go to things like grass but also thicker  uh bushes instead of just chops and we  also like to use this one if we really  take on big brumbles  the blade itself the saw blades don't  like it and this one every now and then  if we really do the grass  but yeah this is just standard  information for brush cut just not  specifically for electric ones  so now let's get back to why this  electric one is different than the gas  one all right so here we have the review  of the brush cutter the pros and the  cons  they're pretty similar to the chainsaw  as in it's also less noisy because  electric  it's easier to start and it doesn't use  any gas so you can just run it on  electricity which is cool  the downside is  yeah also hard to repair  you can't really open it up and it  doesn't last super long the battery life  so the endurance is not very high  however the main difference is it does  have enough power it really feels like  it's good and like with the chainsaw  so those were the two reviews of the  brush cutter and the chainsaw now let me  give you a final verdict of both of  these electric machines  all right so here's the final verdict of  these two machines used for one year in  our setting  so big plus for us is that they just  make less noise because we're here with  many people 